  "final_text": "I submitted **part-based** with 0.88 confidence. I used 24 of the 60 budget points.\n\n**Design:** I trained the learner on two tasks, L1-S1-M1 and L2-S2-M2. I then picked test tasks where the two hypotheses predict opposite results. \"Overlap\" below is the share of inputs where a test task gives the same response as the closer of the two trained tasks.\n\n| Test task | New rules | Overlap | Examples needed | Mean |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| L2-S1-M1 | 0 | 0.06 | 13, 16, 10, 1, 13 | ~11 |\n| L2-S3-M3 | 2 | 0.00 | 6, 26, 36 | ~23 |\n| L4-S4-M4 | 3 | 0.56 | 29, 37, 45, 45, 37 | ~39 |\n| L3-S3-M3 | 3 | 0.06 | 39, 38, 43 | ~40 |\n\n**Evidence:**\n- **Rules reused, outputs different:** L2-S1-M1 was learned fastest even though its outputs barely overlap with either trained task.\n- **Same new-rule count, different overlap:** L4-S4-M4 shares 56% of its outputs with a trained task and L3-S3-M3 almost none. Both had 3 new rules and both cost about 40 examples, so output similarity made no visible difference.\n- **Steps with new rules:** the 2-new-rule task fell in between.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- **Noise:** results are noisy. Several tests returned a value of 1, and the two control tasks (L1-S2-M1 and L2-S2-M4) gave inconsistent results. The main contrast held up across repeats.\n- **Hidden similarity measure:** I used output overlap from `compare` as a stand-in for the holistic learner's similarity measure. If its measure is based on which rules tasks share rather than on their outputs, it would produce this same pattern. That is why I stopped at 0.88 confidence rather than going higher.",
